# Template for .env.production, which is NOT committed. Copy this to
# `.env.production` and fill in the three values before running `npm run build`.
#
# WHY THE REAL FILE IS NOT IN THE REPO
#
# Not because these are secrets. A browser app has to ship its user pool and client id
# in order to sign anyone in, so they are readable in the bundle by anyone who can load
# the page, and the client is deliberately configured with no secret - see
# cdk/lib/cognito_stack.py. They are kept out because they are deployment specifics on
# a public remote, which is a different argument and a weaker one, but it is the call
# that was made.
#
# WHY A FILE AND NOT SHELL EXPORTS
#
# So the deployed build cannot quietly lose its login screen. `npm run build` picks a
# file up with no operator action; leaving it to the environment means one forgotten
# export ships a bundle where isAuthConfigured is false and the app renders with no
# sign-in at all - which still "works" against CloudFront, because API Gateway would
# 401 every call, so it presents as a broken API rather than a missing login.
#
# That failure mode is now MORE likely, not less, because a fresh clone starts without
# the file. If you have just cloned this repo: copy this file before you build, and if
# the sign-in screen is missing from a build, this is the first thing to check.
#
# Deliberately NOT a plain `.env`, which would also apply to `npm run dev`. Local
# development runs against `uvicorn` with no API Gateway authorizer in front of it.
#
# The deployed dev values are in the CognitoStack outputs:
#   aws cloudformation describe-stacks \
#     --stack-name PlanningRoadmapdevCognitoStack<suffix> \
#     --region ca-central-1 --query 'Stacks[0].Outputs'
# The pool is shared with the marketing tool; the client id is this app's own.

VITE_COGNITO_USER_POOL_ID=ca-central-1_xxxxxxxxx
VITE_COGNITO_CLIENT_ID=xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
VITE_AWS_REGION=ca-central-1
